## What this cycle did (S504): wired batched wiring into the chain + resolved the fast-path sign flip

The recorded NEXT ACTION was the end-to-end integration of the S503 batched-wiring primitive into `run_chain`, then a re-measure with `FAST_BIG=True`. Both done.

**Integration (defer-and-batch).** Added two entry points to `batched_wiring.py`:
- `chain_obligation(...)` — builds one wiring obligation from a deferred chain check (prover-supplied claim, honest-or-lying chain over the shared `l_max` cube),
- `verify_wiring_obligations(...)` — discharges a list of obligations in one batched chain.

In `compressed_layer.py` I threaded a `defer` accumulator through `large_reduce`/`verify_affine_region`/`verify_waff_value` (large affine, `accept_rem=p−1`) and `small_reduce` (small division, `accept_rem=None`). With `run_chain(batch_wiring=True)` (delegate only), each layer **appends** its wiring obligation instead of calling `inner_verify_div` inline; all `2K−1` obligations discharge in one batched chain after the loop. The `verify_waff_value` `[e≤ecut]` comparator fold stays per-layer (p-independent), as the action specified. Default `batch_wiring=False` keeps every prior artifact byte-for-byte.

**Verification** (`compressed_layer.py` selftest §19/§19b, both green): chain verdict unchanged (honest == sieve; `delta_pi` + self-consistent liar rejected) over `q` & `BIG_Q`, structured & dense, alone and composed with `batch_trace`; the wiring-specific liars (`small_forge`, `small_chain`, `waff_chain`, `waff_forge`) all rejected **through** the batched discharge; `batch_wiring` without `delegate` raises.

**Headline measurement** (`--n 16 --bench-combined`, BIG_Q, delegate+structured):

| config | wall (ms) | comm | vs base |
|---|---|---|---|
| baseline (no batch, obj) | 17075 | 87226 | 1.00× |
| batch_trace (obj) | 13998 | 85554 | 1.22× |
| batch_trace+wiring (obj) | 12784 | **16509** | 1.34× |
| batch_trace (FAST) | 25452 | 85554 | 0.67× ← **S502 loss reproduced** |
| **batch_trace+wiring (FAST)** | **8873** | **16509** | **1.92×** |

The fast Mersenne path was a *loss* with only the trace batched (tiny per-layer wiring cubes penalize the 24-op mulmod) and becomes a **1.92× net win** once both kernels are widened — well under the 15.5 s target. Wiring batch also cuts communication **5.3×**.

**Item-5 delivered.** In the winning config, `claimed π == sieve` over the sound-characteristic `BIG_Q=2⁶¹−1`: n=16 π=6542 (9.3 s), n=18 π=23000 (22 s), **n=20 π(1048575)=82025 (66 s)** — exact π at x≈10⁶ behind an Õ(√x) verifier, Õ(x) prover, both wirings delegated.

**Honest scope:** this is the verification/certification line, not the computation goal — verifier is Õ(√x), prover Õ(x), and leaf openings are still `mle_eval` stand-ins (no `BREAKTHROUGH.md`). I set the **NEXT ACTION** to building a real multilinear polynomial-commitment opening to replace those stand-ins — the last O(√x)-per-open term keeping the Õ(√x)-verifier claim from being unconditional.

